World jogs of X, Y and Z along the tilted work plane and along the tool.

5axiskins with a 100 mm pivot, the tool centre point type, Y slow and
short against X and Z.  The plane frame: a plane defined under an XY
rotation, incremental jogs of each letter, each world move checked
against the plane's axes as status reports them turned by the XY
rotation, rotaries untouched; a machine jog afterwards starts where the
frame jogs left the machine.  A plane whose X runs mostly along Y: the
jog along it runs at the speed Y allows.  One frame axis moves at a
time: a jog of plane Y asked for while plane X runs waits until X has
stopped.  A continuous jog stops where the turned axis meets the
[AXIS_Y] travel without a soft limit fault, plane Y asked for at once
too, and no further jog leaves it.  The frame cannot change while a jog
is under way.  The tool frame with the head laid over and turned: the
jogs follow the tool frame status reports, and a plane in effect leaves
that frame as the kinematics reports it.  Under the identity type the tool frame is the machine's.  halui's
jog-frame pins select the frame and report it.  Every servo cycle of
every jog is sampled: no world axis goes over its own speed or
acceleration.  The whole test runs on the trapezoid and on the S-curve
planner.  Then trivkins, not homed, with a plane turned 45 degrees about
Z: a frame jog gets the travel a plain jog of an axis not homed gets,
one range either way of where it stands, not the [AXIS_<letter>]
limits, so an incremental jog past them goes and a continuous one runs
to one range from its start.

Then a trunnion, xyzac-trt-kins, on the tool centre point type with C at
90 degrees: a machine frame jog moves the slide of its letter alone, X,
Y and Z, and again with A tilted 30 degrees, since the machine does not
turn when the table does; the plane frame with no plane jogs the part's
X, turned with the table onto the Y slide; the tool frame jogs Z on the
Z slide; on the identity type a machine X jog moves X.
